/*
# WrappingLayout — a flow that wraps to new rows

A horizontal flow that **wraps**: children run left to right `spacing` apart,
and when the next one would overflow the view's width it drops to a new row,
`rowSpacing` down. With room for
one row it is a plain row; as the view narrows it stacks — cards that reflow
on a phone with no media query. It owns both axes of each child (a flow is
2-D); sizes stay the children's own. `justify`
sets each finished row along the flow — `start`, `center`, `end`, or `fill`,
which spreads a row's slack into its gaps the way justified text does;
`indent` and `hangingIndent` inset the first row and every row after it, the
two shapes a paragraph takes. A child with `flexes = true` takes the rest of the
row it lands on: it wraps by its content width, then grows into what the row
has left, or gives up what the row is short — a strip of filters that fills
the room its row's other children leave, and scrolls when that is too little.
A weight (`flexes = 2`) and a range (`minWidth`, `maxWidth`) share a row as they
share a `SimpleLayout` run, and a child with `shrinks = true` gives up width when
its row is too long. A flexing child with a `minWidth` is a **cell**: it wraps by
its minimum, the cells of a row share its room, and on the last row they keep the
width of the row above, so cells of one minimum are equal columns that reflow
with the room.

Division of labor: this is the tool for uniform, replicated collections
that reflow by fitting. Managing named regions per width tier is
ResponsiveLayout's.

Written in Declare over the Layout kernel — the whole strategy is place().

**Padding is the view's** (`View.padding`), and a flow answers to it on
both axes: rows **wrap at the content width** — the room between the left and
right insets, never the view's own width — and a view that sizes itself from
the flow grows by the vertical pair, so no row runs under the inset.

```declare
box: View [ x = 20, y = 20, width = 260,
    layout: WrappingLayout [ spacing = 8, rowSpacing = 8 ],
    View [ width = 70, height = 26, cornerRadius = 6, fill = steelblue ],
    View [ width = 110, height = 26, cornerRadius = 6, fill = cadetblue ],
    View [ width = 90, height = 26, cornerRadius = 6, fill = slategray ],
    View [ width = 120, height = 26, cornerRadius = 6, fill = darkslateblue ]
    ]
```

## spacing
The horizontal gap between children in a row.

## rowSpacing
The vertical gap between rows. The two gaps are independent: a uniform grid writes
the same number for both, and a negative `rowSpacing` pulls rows into each other,
which is what a large display face wants when it wraps — the row height a big face
reports is looser than the line wants to sit.

## justify
How a finished row sits along the flow: `start` (the default, left), `center`, `end`
(right), or `fill` — the row's slack is spread evenly into the gaps between its
children, so every full row ends flush at the right edge, as justified text does. As
in text, the last row is left at `start` under `fill` (a ragged last line), and a row
of one child has no gap to widen and stays put. A bare literal.

## indent
The first row's inset from the left, in pixels — a paragraph's first-line indent. The
first row's usable width shrinks by it; later rows are unaffected (see `hangingIndent`).

## hangingIndent
The inset of every row after the first — a hanging indent, the shape of a bibliography
entry or a wrapped signature whose continuation lines step in under the name. The two
compose: `indent` sets where the first row starts, `hangingIndent` where the rest do.

## columns
A grid of equal cells (default `0`, off): every row holds this many children, each in a
cell of the row's room shared out — the content width less `spacing` between them, and the
row's indent. A child with `flexes = true` takes its cell's width; any other keeps its own
and sits at its cell's left. With `columns = { narrow ? 5 : 10 }`, ten options are one row
at a desk and two of five on a phone, every cell the same width.

```declare
scale: View [ width = 100%,
    layout: WrappingLayout [ columns = { app.width < 600 ? 5 : 10 }, spacing = 6, rowSpacing = 6 ],
    View [ datapath = { app.levels[] }, flexes = true, height = 44, cornerRadius = 6, fill = gainsboro ]
    ]
```

## align
Where each child sits **within its row**: `start` (the default — the row's top),
`center`, `end`, or `baseline`, which sits every child in the row on one baseline — a
`Text` reports its own, a composite **declares** which part carries it
(`baseline: number = { cap.y + cap.baseline }`), and a child that declares none is
refused by name and placed at the row's top. A row's height grows to hold the
baseline-shifted children. When the flow **never wrapped** — one row, which is the whole
content — `center` and `end` align it in the view's own height instead, so a single row
in a `height = 90` bar really sits in the middle of it; a view that derives its height
from this flow keeps the row as the band. A bare literal.

## place()
Runs the flow, wrapping when the next child would overflow the view's width. **It owns
both axes of each child** — a flow is 2-D — while sizes stay the children's own. A child
never wraps when it is first in its row, so one over-wide child overflows rather than
looping forever. Each finished row starts at its indent, is justified along the flow, and
has its children aligned across it, in one pass over the row.
*/

class WrappingLayout extends Layout [
    spacing: number = 0,
    rowSpacing: number = 0,
    justify: Justify = start,
    align: CrossAlign = start,
    indent: number = 0,
    hangingIndent: number = 0,
    columns: number = 0,

    place() {
        // the room to wrap in — the view's content box, its width less its own padding
        const w = this.contentExtent("width")
        const sp = this.spacing
        const ls = this.rowSpacing
        const jf = this.justify
        const al = this.align
        const ind0 = Math.max(0, this.indent)
        const indN = Math.max(0, this.hangingIndent)
        const kids = this.laid()
        const out = [] as any
        const heights = [] as any   // per child, for the cross pass over its row
        const bases = [] as any     // per child: its declared baseline, or null
        const natural = [] as any   // per child: a flexing child's own content width, else null
        let cx = ind0
        let cy = 0
        let rowH = 0
        let rowBase = 0
        let firstInRow = true
        let rowStart = 0
        // `columns`: a grid of equal cells — every row holds that many children,
        // each in a cell of the row's room shared out (its indent left out)
        const cols = Math.max(0, Math.floor(this.columns))
        const cellOf = (inset) => cols > 0 ? Math.max(0, (w - inset - (cols - 1) * sp) / cols) : 0
        let inRow = 0
        // what a run gives its children along the flow (SimpleLayout's model):
        // a child that flexes (a weight above 0) or shrinks has its width
        // written here, starting from `natural`; every size stays in the
        // child's range (minWidth, maxWidth; 0 = no limit)
        const weight = (c) => {
            const f = (c as any).flexes
            return f === true ? 1 : (typeof f == "number" && f > 0 ? f : 0)
            }
        const shrinking = (c) => weight(c) == 0 && (c as any).shrinks === true
        const clamp = (c, v) => {
            const hi = (c as any).maxWidth
            return Math.max(hi > 0 ? Math.min(v, hi) : v, (c as any).minWidth, 0)
            }
        // per weight, what the last full row's flexing children grew by: cells
        // that state a minWidth keep it on the last row, so they line up
        let grewBy = null
        // a finished row [rowStart, end): justify it along the flow, align its
        // children across it, and answer the row's final height — which grows
        // under baseline alignment, since children shift down to the line.
        // `rowW` is where the row's content ends (its indent included); `last`
        // says whether it is the flow's final row, which `fill` leaves ragged.
        const finish = (end, rowW, last) => {
            const slack = Math.max(0, w - rowW)
            // a row too long for the room: its shrinking children give way in
            // proportion to their size, down to their minimums; then its flexing
            // children share the slack, or the shortfall still left, by weight,
            // within their ranges — and what follows each one moves along
            const given = [] as any
            const flexers = [] as any
            const shrinkers = [] as any
            for (let j = rowStart; j < end; j++) {
                given.push(natural[j])
                if (!kids[j].visible || natural[j] == null) continue
                if (weight(kids[j]) > 0) flexers.push(j)
                else shrinkers.push(j)
            }
            let over = rowW - w
            if (over > 0 && shrinkers.length > 0) {
                const held = shrinkers.map((j) => false)
                for (let round = 0; round <= shrinkers.length && over > 0; round = round + 1) {
                    let basis = 0
                    shrinkers.forEach((j, k) => { if (!held[k]) basis = basis + given[j - rowStart] })
                    if (basis <= 0) break
                    let again = false
                    shrinkers.forEach((j, k) => {
                        if (held[k]) return
                        const n = given[j - rowStart]
                        const lo = (kids[j] as any).minWidth
                        if (n - over * n / basis < lo) { over = over - (n - lo); given[j - rowStart] = lo; held[k] = true; again = true }
                        })
                    if (!again) {
                        shrinkers.forEach((j, k) => { if (!held[k]) { const n = given[j - rowStart]; given[j - rowStart] = n - over * n / basis } })
                        over = 0
                        }
                }
            }
            if (flexers.length > 0) {
                const carry = last && rowStart > 0 && grewBy != null && flexers.every((j) => (kids[j] as any).minWidth > 0)
                let free = -over
                const held = flexers.map((j) => false)
                for (let round = 0; round <= flexers.length; round = round + 1) {
                    let total = 0
                    flexers.forEach((j, k) => { if (!held[k]) total = total + weight(kids[j]) })
                    if (total <= 0) break
                    const per = carry ? grewBy : free / total
                    if (!last) grewBy = per
                    let off = 0
                    flexers.forEach((j, k) => {
                        if (held[k]) return
                        const want = natural[j] + per * weight(kids[j])
                        given[j - rowStart] = clamp(kids[j], want)
                        off = off + given[j - rowStart] - want
                        })
                    if (carry || Math.abs(off) < 0.001) break
                    flexers.forEach((j, k) => {
                        if (held[k]) return
                        const want = natural[j] + per * weight(kids[j])
                        if ((off > 0 && given[j - rowStart] > want) || (off < 0 && given[j - rowStart] < want)) { held[k] = true; free = free - (given[j - rowStart] - natural[j]) }
                        })
                }
            }
            if (flexers.length + shrinkers.length > 0) {
                let shift = 0
                for (let j = rowStart; j < end; j++) {
                    if (!kids[j].visible) continue
                    out[j].x = out[j].x + shift
                    if (natural[j] != null) {
                        out[j].w = given[j - rowStart]
                        shift = shift + given[j - rowStart] - natural[j]
                    }
                }
            }
            // a row its flexing children fill has no slack left to justify
            if (flexers.length == 0 && slack > 0 && (jf == "center" || jf == "end")) {
                const off = jf == "center" ? slack / 2 : slack
                for (let j = rowStart; j < end; j++) out[j].x = out[j].x + off
            } else if (flexers.length == 0 && slack > 0 && jf == "fill" && !last) {
                // spread the slack into the gaps: the k-th visible child after
                // the row's first moves by k shares
                let n = 0
                for (let j = rowStart; j < end; j++) if (kids[j].visible) n = n + 1
                if (n > 1) {
                    const share = slack / (n - 1)
                    let k = 0
                    for (let j = rowStart; j < end; j++) { if (!kids[j].visible) continue; out[j].x = out[j].x + share * k; k = k + 1 }
                }
            }
            let lineH = rowH
            if (al == "baseline") {
                lineH = 0
                for (let j = rowStart; j < end; j++) {
                    if (!kids[j].visible) continue
                    const top = bases[j] == null ? 0 : rowBase - bases[j]
                    lineH = Math.max(lineH, top + heights[j])
                }
            }
            // A flow that never wrapped is not a row inside a stack of rows —
            // it IS the view's content, so center/end align it in the view's
            // own cross extent (`viewExtent` answers 0 when that extent is
            // measured from these children, and the row height stands). With
            // two or more rows the band is the row, as a wrapped flow's must
            // be: there is no `align-content` here to spread the leftover.
            if (rowStart == 0 && last && (al == "center" || al == "end")) lineH = Math.max(lineH, this.viewExtent("height"))
            for (let j = rowStart; j < end; j++) {
                if (!kids[j].visible) continue
                let off = 0
                if (al == "center") off = (lineH - heights[j]) / 2
                else if (al == "end") off = lineH - heights[j]
                else if (al == "baseline") {
                    if (bases[j] != null) off = rowBase - bases[j]
                    else this.refuseBaseline(kids[j])
                }
                out[j].y = out[j].y + off
            }
            return lineH
        }
        for (let i = 0; i < kids.length; i++) {
            const c = kids[i]
            if (!c.visible) { out.push({ x: cx, y: cy }); heights.push(0); bases.push(null); natural.push(null); continue }   // skipped: reclaim its slot
            // the child's footprint (scale/rotation composed, position-free —
            // place() must not read the x/y it writes), and the transform's own
            // lead offset, compensated so the visible box lands on the flow.
            // A flexing child's width is this flow's to write, so it is fitted
            // by its content width and its footprint is never read.
            const flex = weight(c) > 0 || shrinking(c)
            const f = flex ? null : (c as any).footprint()
            const ch = flex ? (c as any).height : f.height
            const leadX = flex ? 0 : f.x
            const leadY = flex ? 0 : f.y
            // under `columns` a child takes a cell: a flexing one is the cell's
            // width, a shrinking one its own up to the cell's, and the next row
            // starts after the last column, whatever fits. Outside a grid a
            // flexing child wraps by its minWidth when it states one (a cell),
            // else by its content's width, as a shrinking one does
            const own = flex ? clamp(c, weight(c) > 0 && (c as any).minWidth > 0 ? (c as any).minWidth : (c as any).contentWidth) : 0
            let cw = cols > 0 ? 0 : flex ? own : f.width
            natural.push(flex && cols == 0 ? cw : null)
            // wrap when this child (with its leading gap) would overflow — never
            // on a row's first child, so one over-wide child just overflows
            if (!firstInRow && (cols > 0 ? inRow >= cols : cx + sp + cw > w)) { const lh = finish(i, cx, false); cx = indN; cy = cy + lh + ls; rowH = 0; rowBase = 0; firstInRow = true; rowStart = i; inRow = 0 }
            const cell = cols > 0 ? cellOf(rowStart == 0 ? ind0 : indN) : 0
            if (cols > 0) cw = cell
            if (!firstInRow) cx = cx + sp
            out.push(cols > 0 && flex ? { x: cx, y: cy, w: clamp(c, weight(c) > 0 ? cell : Math.min(own, cell)) } : { x: cx - leadX, y: cy - leadY })
            inRow = inRow + 1
            heights.push(ch)
            const b = (c as any).baseline
            bases.push(typeof b == "number" ? b : null)
            if (al == "baseline" && typeof b == "number") rowBase = Math.max(rowBase, b)
            cx = cx + cw
            rowH = Math.max(rowH, ch)
            firstInRow = false
        }
        finish(kids.length, cx, true)   // the last (or only) row — ragged under fill
        return out
        }
    ]
